''[[Modern Family]]'' is an American television [[sitcom]] created by [[Steven Levitan]] and [[Christopher Lloyd (TV producer)|Christopher Lloyd]] and aired by the [[American Broadcasting Company]] (ABC). The show premiered on September 23, 2009, and ended on April 8, 2020, after [[List of Modern Family episodes|250 episodes spanning eleven seasons]]. It stars [[Ed O'Neill]], [[Sofía Vergara]], [[Julie Bowen]], [[Ty Burrell]], [[Jesse Tyler Ferguson]], [[Eric Stonestreet]], [[Sarah Hyland]], [[Ariel Winter]], [[Nolan Gould]], and [[Rico Rodriguez (actor)|Rico Rodriguez]], with [[Aubrey Anderson-Emmons]], [[Jeremy Maguire]], and [[Reid Ewing]] joining the main cast in later seasons.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thefutoncritic.com/showatch/modern-family/ |title=''Modern Family'' – Showatch |website=[[The Futon Critic]] |access-date=May 13, 2023}}</ref> The show uses a [[mockumentary]] format to follow three related families: the [[nuclear family]] of Phil, Claire, and their children Haley, Alex, and Luke; Jay and Gloria, a [[Stepfamily|blended family]] with Gloria's son Manny; and [[Same-sex relationship|gay partners]] Mitchell and Cameron, who adopt a daughter, Lily.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thefutoncritic.com/reviews/2009/06/02/the-futons-first-look-modern-family-abc-31545/20090602_modernfamily/ |title=The Futon's First Look: ''Modern Family'' (ABC) |last=Sullivan |first=Brian Ford |date=June 2, 2009 |website=[[The Futon Critic]] |access-date=May 13, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.sfgate.com/news/article/Review-Modern-Family-Cougar-Town-3285979.php |title=Review: ''Modern Family'', ''Cougar Town'' |last=Goodman |first=Tim |date=September 21, 2009 |website=[[SFGate]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=April 8, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230408124523/https://www.sfgate.com/news/article/Review-Modern-Family-Cougar-Town-3285979.php |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.salon.com/2010/11/14/modern_family_king_of_the_sitcom/ |title=''Modern Family'': Best comedy on TV |last=Havrilesky |first=Heather |date=November 14, 2010 |website=[[Salon.com|Salon]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=July 23, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210723184709/https://www.salon.com/2010/11/14/modern_family_king_of_the_sitcom/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

''Modern Family'' was acclaimed during its run for its humor and portrayal of gay relationships, and it was credited with a resurgence in network sitcoms after its debut.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/04/18/arts/television/18sitcoms.html |title=In Bear Times, a Bull Market for Sitcoms |last=Stelter |first=Brian |date=April 16, 2010 |website=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |url-access=limited |archive-date=January 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126025321/http://www.nytimes.com/2010/04/18/arts/television/18sitcoms.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/you-might-not-care-about-modern-family-anymore-but-it-helped-change-tv/2019/02/07/c6907130-2b0f-11e9-b011-d8500644dc98_story.html |title=You might not care about ''Modern Family'' anymore, but it helped change TV |last=Yahr |first=Emily |date=February 8, 2019 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |url-access=limited |archive-date=November 27, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221127054114/https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/you-might-not-care-about-modern-family-anymore-but-it-helped-change-tv/2019/02/07/c6907130-2b0f-11e9-b011-d8500644dc98_story.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://ew.com/tv/modern-family-series-finale-review/ |title=''Modern Family''{{'}}s series finale is a feel-good farewell to a comedy pioneer: Review |last=Baldwin |first=Kristen |date=April 8, 2020 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=December 2, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221202005603/https://ew.com/tv/modern-family-series-finale-review/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/abc-s-modern-family-finale-end-era-hopefully-beginning-new-ncna1179366 |title=ABC's ''Modern Family'' finale is the end of an era — and hopefully the beginning of a new one |last=Majors |first=Steve |date=April 8, 2020 |publisher=[[NBC News]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=December 27, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221227092857/https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/abc-s-modern-family-finale-end-era-hopefully-beginning-new-ncna1179366 |url-status=live }}</ref> The show won twenty-two [[Primetime Emmy Awards]] from eighty-five nominations, including [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Comedy Series|Outstanding Comedy Series]] five consecutive times from 2010 to 2014, tied with ''[[Frasier]]'' for most wins in the category.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/emmy-awards-2014-modern-family-727178/ |title=Emmys: ''Modern Family'' Wins Outstanding Comedy Series |last1=Goldberg |first1=Lesley |last2=Youn |first2=Soo |date=August 25, 2014 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |access-date=May 13, 2023 |archive-date=September 30, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220930065552/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/emmy-awards-2014-modern-family-727178/ |url-status=live }}</ref> It also won four [[Producers Guild of America Award for Best Episodic Comedy|Producers Guild of America Awards for Outstanding Producer of Episodic Television, Comedy]] and two [[GLAAD Media Award for Outstanding Comedy Series|GLAAD Media Awards for Outstanding Comedy Series]]. From critics' organizations, the show received a [[Golden Globe Award for Best Television Series – Musical or Comedy]], a [[Critics' Choice Television Award for Best Comedy Series]], and two [[TCA Award for Outstanding Achievement in Comedy|TCA Awards for Outstanding Achievement in Comedy]]. ''Modern Family'' was named one of the top television programs of the year four times by the [[American Film Institute]] and received a [[Peabody Award]] in 2010.

O'Neill, Vergara, Bowen, Burrell, Ferguson, and Stonestreet were each nominated for multiple Emmys for their performances. They ultimately won a combined six Emmys – four for [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Supporting Actor in a Comedy Series|Supporting Actor in a Comedy Series]] (two for Stonestreet and two for Burrell) and two for [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Supporting Actress in a Comedy Series|Supporting Actress in a Comedy Series]] (for Bowen). In addition, the cast won five [[Screen Actors Guild Awards]] – four for [[Screen Actors Guild Award for Outstanding Performance by an Ensemble in a Comedy Series|Outstanding Performance by an Ensemble in a Comedy Series]] and one for [[Screen Actors Guild Award for Outstanding Performance by a Male Actor in a Comedy Series|Outstanding Performance by a Male Actor in a Comedy Series]] (for Burrell). The show's directors received four Emmys and two [[Directors Guild of America Awards]], while its writers received six [[Writers Guild of America Awards]] as well as two Emmys.

